          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            is called
            %em logarithmic
            (LCFT) if it looks like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+conformal+field+theory"} rational conformal field theory
            except that some of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} 2-point functions
            may have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logarithm"} logarithmic
            dependence on the field insertion, in addition to the usual power law (see, e.g.
            %a{:href => "#FuchsSchweigert19"} Fuchs &amp; Schweigert 2019, (8)
            for a clear account).
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            A classical example of a logarithmic CFT it the “triple algebra of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c=-2
            Virasoso model”, see the references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ExampleTripletAlgebra"} below
          %p
            More recently it was understood (
            %a{:href => "#Gaberdiel01"}> Gaberdiel 2001
            , review in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Ridout10"} Ridout 2010
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Ridout20"} 2020
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+models"} WZW models
            at “admissible”
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} fractional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+%28Chern-Simons+theory%29"} levels
            are examples of logarithmic CFTs, see the references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FractionalLevelWZWModelReferences}"} further below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            at non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"}> integer
            \-level does not have a geometric
            = succeed "-description" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+algebra"} current algebra
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
          %p
            In fact, the triple algebra
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c=-2
            = succeed "-WZW" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/su%282%29"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔰𝔲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{su}(2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0
            (
